---
import ComparisonPageLayout from '../../layouts/ComparisonPageLayout.astro';

// The Kiro CLI claims changed in this refresh were checked against kiro.dev
// docs, pricing, license and changelog on 2026-10-11; sources are in the
// verification block below.
const comparison = {
  competitor: {
    name: 'Kiro CLI',
    icon: '👻',
  },
  tldr: {
    title: 'TL;DR',
    points: [
      'Kiro CLI (formerly Amazon Q Developer CLI) is closed source. The old Amazon Q CLI repo is open source but gets only critical security fixes.',
      'Kiro runs its models on Amazon Bedrock and needs a sign-in. Its docs list no local-model or bring-your-own-endpoint option. With its default embedded backend, Caro runs a local model and needs no account.',
      'Kiro asks before most shell commands and lets you write deny/ask/allow rules. Its docs describe no built-in list of dangerous commands. Caro checks every command against 52+ curated patterns with risk levels.',
      'Kiro is a full coding agent with MCP, custom agents and hooks. Caro does one job: natural language to a safe shell command.',
      'Caro is free and open source. Kiro paid plans start at $20 per month.'
    ]
  },
  headToHead: [
    { feature: 'Open source', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'Works 100% offline', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'Local models', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'No account required', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'Built-in dangerous-command patterns', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'Risk level for each command', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'Custom command rules (patterns, allow/deny lists)', caro: true, competitor: true, winner: 'tie' },
    { feature: 'Uses your existing terminal', caro: true, competitor: true, winner: 'tie' },
    { feature: 'MCP tool integration', caro: false, competitor: true, winner: 'competitor' },
    { feature: 'Custom agents', caro: false, competitor: true, winner: 'competitor' },
    { feature: 'AWS service integration', caro: false, competitor: true, winner: 'competitor' },
  ],
  sections: [
    {
      title: 'Cloud Dependency',
      icon: '$',
      content: `
        <p><strong>Kiro CLI</strong> sends every request to models hosted on Amazon Bedrock: Claude, GPT and open-weight models. It needs a sign-in with GitHub, Google, AWS Builder ID or IAM Identity Center. It does not need an AWS account. It has no local-model or custom-endpoint option today.</p>
        <p><strong>Caro</strong>, with its default embedded backend, runs a local model on your machine. After a one-time model download it needs no internet, no account and no API key, on any cloud or on bare metal.</p>
      `
    },
    {
      title: 'Privacy and Data',
      icon: '▸',
      content: `
        <p><strong>Kiro CLI</strong> collects usage data and metrics by default for free-tier and individual users, and can use content to improve the service. You can opt out. Enterprise users signed in through IAM Identity Center are opted out automatically.</p>
        <p><strong>Caro</strong>, with its default embedded backend, processes prompts and commands locally. They do not leave your machine. Optional remote backends send prompts to the server you choose.</p>
      `
    },
    {
      title: 'Safety Approach',
      icon: '■',
      content: `
        <p><strong>Kiro CLI</strong> runs read-only commands like <code>git status</code> and <code>pwd</code> on its own and asks before everything else. It splits compound commands on <code>;</code>, <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, <code>||</code> and <code>|</code> and checks each part. You can write deny/ask/allow rules in <code>permissions.yaml</code>, and admins can enforce them. <code>--trust-all-tools</code> turns the prompts off for a session. Its docs describe no curated list of dangerous commands.</p>
        <p><strong>Caro</strong> checks every command against 52+ curated patterns and rates it Safe, Moderate, High or Critical. At the default safety level it blocks Critical commands, such as <code>rm -rf /</code> and fork bombs, with no configuration, and asks before High ones. You can add your own patterns and allowlist entries.</p>
      `
    },
    {
      title: 'Features and Scope',
      icon: '▸',
      content: `
        <p><strong>Kiro CLI</strong> is a full coding agent: interactive chat, custom agents, MCP servers, hooks, headless mode for CI and the Agent Client Protocol. It shares a subscription with the Kiro IDE.</p>
        <p><strong>Caro</strong> specializes in shell commands, with POSIX-first output, platform detection and GNU/BSD awareness. It works the same on AWS, GCP, Azure or bare metal.</p>
      `
    },
  ],
  verdict: {
    chooseCaro: [
      'You need offline or air-gapped capability',
      'You want no account and no cloud dependency',
      'You want a built-in safety library with risk levels',
      'You work across clouds or on-premise',
      'You prefer free, open-source tools',
    ],
    chooseCompetitor: [
      'You want a full coding agent in the terminal',
      'You need MCP integration and custom agents',
      'You already use the Kiro IDE or the AWS ecosystem',
      'You want hosted frontier models',
    ]
  },
  verification: {
    date: '2026-10-11',
    sources: [
      { label: 'Kiro docs: models', url: 'https://kiro.dev/docs/models/' },
      { label: 'Kiro docs: permissions', url: 'https://kiro.dev/docs/permissions/' },
      { label: 'Kiro docs: data protection', url: 'https://kiro.dev/docs/privacy-and-security/data-protection/' },
      { label: 'Kiro license', url: 'https://kiro.dev/license/' },
      { label: 'Kiro pricing', url: 'https://kiro.dev/pricing/' },
      { label: 'aws/amazon-q-developer-cli README', url: 'https://github.com/aws/amazon-q-developer-cli' },
    ]
  }
};
---

<ComparisonPageLayout
  title="Caro vs Kiro CLI - Feature Comparison | Shell AI Tools"
  description="Compare Caro and Kiro CLI (formerly Amazon Q CLI): an open-source, local-by-default shell CLI with built-in safety patterns vs a cloud-hosted coding agent."
  comparison={comparison}
  heroSubtitle="Open, offline shell companion vs cloud-hosted coding agent"
  finalCtaHeading="Cloud-Agnostic. Privacy-First."
  finalCtaSubtext="Works on any infrastructure. No account, no cloud dependency."
/>
